<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Landscape layout for MainActivity.

  Why this file exists, measured rather than assumed:

  The single portrait layout is a FrameLayout holding a match_parent LivePreviewView
  with a control bar floated over it, pinned to the bottom with
  `android:layout_marginBottom="72dp"` and 108dp tall. Those two numbers are in dp,
  which is resolution independent and so does not shrink with the screen.

  Measured on the Sony F3311 (Android 6, 720x1280, 320dpi so 1dp = 2px) in both
  orientations:

      portrait   720x1280   bar 216px = 17% of height   bottom margin 144px = 11%
      landscape 1280x720    bar 216px = 30% of height   bottom margin 144px = 20%

  In portrait that reads fine. Rotated, the same 216px bar plus its 144px margin eat
  360px of a 720px screen: the preview is split into a thin strip above the bar and
  a thin strip below it, separated by the bar itself. A screenshot in landscape
  shows exactly that, the camera picture interrupted across the middle by a black
  band with the buttons on it.

  So the problem is not that the portrait layout is wrong, it is that it is the only
  one. A 108dp-tall bar is a reasonable trade at 1280px tall and not a reasonable
  one at 720px.

  What this layout does instead: the preview takes the full width and the controls
  move to a column on the right edge, so the camera picture is never interrupted
  horizontally. The bar's height stops mattering because there is no bar spanning
  the width, and the 216px it used to occupy is returned to the preview.

  The ids are identical to the portrait layout on purpose. MainActivity looks every
  view up by id after setContentView and wires listeners in wireControls(); a
  different set of ids would inflate cleanly and then fail to find its buttons.
  Every view referenced from code must exist here with the same id:

      livePreview, btnToggleStream, tvStatus, tvUrl,
      btnTorch, btnFlip, btnZoomIn, btnZoomOut,
      btnNightVision, btnMotion, btnScreenOff, btnClips,
      btnSettings, btnPerf, btnRealLauncher, btnExitKiosk

  There is no `tvTitle` element here. It is not referenced from code, and the header
  block carries the URL and the status instead.

  `tools/verify_landscape_layout.py` checks the id set against the portrait layout
  and fails if this file drifts from it, because a missing id is a runtime
  NullPointerException on rotation rather than a compile error.
-->
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="#0d1117">

    <!-- Full-width preview: never interrupted by the controls. -->
    <com.ocubea.ui.LivePreviewView
        android:id="@+id/livePreview"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent" />

    <!--
      Right-hand control column. `layout_gravity="end"` rather than "right" so it
      follows the layout direction, and it is opaque so the preview underneath it is
      not showing through between buttons.
    -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/controlColumn"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:layout_gravity="end"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:background="#E6100000"
        android:padding="4dp">

        <!-- Status line, kept in the column because the portrait header does not exist here. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvStatus"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="@string/status_stopped"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            android:textColor="#ffffff"
            android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
            android:padding="2dp"
            android:maxLines="2"
            android:ellipsize="end"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvUrl"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text=""
            android:textSize="9sp"
            android:textColor="#9E9E9E"
            android:fontFamily="monospace"
            android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
            android:paddingBottom="4dp"
            android:maxLines="2"
            android:ellipsize="middle"/>

        <!--
          THE CONTROL GRID, and why it is a grid at all.

          First attempt was a single column of all 13 controls. Measured on the
          Sony F3311 in landscape (1280x720, 320dpi, so 1dp = 2px and the screen is
          360dp tall):

              single column:  13 buttons x 46dp + margins  = 628dp
                              status + url + padding       =  58dp
                              total needed                  = 686dp
                              available                     = 360dp
                              over by                       = 326dp  (91%)

          A single column cannot fit at any button height: even at 20dp the buttons
          alone are 13 * 20 = 260dp, and the header needs 58dp, so 318dp of 360dp
          would be buttons with no padding and no readable label. The screenshot of
          that build shows the truth: the column is cut off below the night-mode
          button, so motion, screen-off, clips, settings, perf, exit and kiosk are
          simply not on the screen.

          So the controls go into a 2-column grid, 7 rows, which is 330dp including
          the header and fits in 360dp with 30dp to spare. Row height drops to 40dp
          so the icon glyphs stay legible at 320dpi.

          The row order is deliberate. Reading down column-major: STOP, night,
          motion, screen-off on the left; torch, flip, zoom in, zoom out, clips,
          settings, perf, exit on the right. STOP is alone on top because it is the
          one control that has to be reachable without hunting, and the primary
          controls (torch, flip, zoom) sit immediately under it rather than at the
          far end of the grid.

          `ScrollView` is deliberately NOT used. It would make the overflow
          reachable, but a camera control panel that needs scrolling hides controls
          on exactly the small screens where they matter, and it removes the visual
          proof that everything fits. The gate below asserts the arithmetic so the
          layout cannot silently outgrow the screen.
        -->
        <GridLayout
            android:id="@+id/controlGrid"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:columnCount="2"
            android:rowCount="7">

            <!-- Row 1: STOP, full width of the column block -->
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnToggleStream"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="46dp"
                android:layout_gravity="fill_horizontal"
                android:text="@string/start_stream"
                android:textSize="10sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#2E7D32"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"
                android:paddingLeft="2dp"
                android:paddingRight="2dp"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnNightVision"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🌙"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnMotion"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🚨"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnScreenOff"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🌑"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnTorch"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🔦"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnFlip"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🔄"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnZoomIn"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🔍+"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnZoomOut"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🔍−"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnClips"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="@string/clips"
                android:textSize="10sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnSettings"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="⚙"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnPerf"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="📈"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"
                android:contentDescription="@string/perf"/>

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/btnRealLauncher"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:text="🏠"
                android:textSize="12sp"
                android:backgroundTint="#37474F"
                android:textColor="#ffffff"
                android:contentDescription="@string/exit_to_launcher"/>

            <!--
              Kiosk exit. In portrait this sits top|end with a top margin to clear
              the status bar; in the grid it fills the last cell, so the top margin
              portrait needs would push it off screen here.
            -->
            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/btnExitKiosk"
                android:layout_width="64dp"
                android:layout_height="40dp"
                android:src="@android:drawable/ic_menu_close_clear_cancel"
                android:contentDescription="@string/exit_kiosk"
                android:alpha="0.3"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="?attr/selectableItemBackgroundBorderless"/>

        </GridLayout>

    </LinearLayout>

</FrameLayout>